"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes \c{mailmap}, a tool for maintaining the
\c{.mailmap} files of git repositories from an identity register. For the
command line interface refer to the \l{mailmap(1)} man page.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

The \c{mailmap} tool maintains the \c{.mailmap} file of a git repository,
which is the file git uses to map the names and emails recorded in the
history to the ones their owners prefer. The mapping itself comes from a
separate file, the identity register, that lists each person along with
the identities they have committed under. A single register can be used
with any number of repositories and this is the case \c{mailmap} was
designed for.

If you have maintained a \c{.mailmap} file by hand, then you are probably
familiar with how it goes: someone commits from a freshly installed machine
or with a new email, their commits start showing up under a new identity,
and sooner or later somebody adds an entry for it. The more repositories a
project has, the harder this becomes to keep up with. An entry gets added
to one repository and forgotten in the next, and it becomes difficult to
tell whether any given \c{.mailmap} is complete. For a project that relies
on its history to establish who wrote what (for example, to generate its
\c{AUTHORS} file), an incomplete \c{.mailmap} means incorrect attribution.

With \c{mailmap} the \c{.mailmap} file is generated. We record the people
and their identities once, in the register, and \c{mailmap} derives the
\c{.mailmap} of each repository from the register and the history of that
repository. If the history contains an identity that the register does not
account for, then \c{mailmap} lists it and fails, which means a generated
\c{.mailmap} is always complete. Before changing anything, \c{mailmap} also
has git apply the generated file to every identity in the history and
makes sure each one maps to the person the register says it belongs to.

We begin with a walkthrough that updates the \c{.mailmap} of a single
repository and then look at the identity register and at what \c{mailmap}
does underneath in more detail.

\h1#start|Getting Started|

Let's start with a repository whose history has two identities for the
same person. Jane made her first commit with the name \c{jane} and her
second one from her old work email:

\
$ cd hello/
$ git log --format='%an <%ae>'
J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
jane <jane@example.org>
\

Git records the author of each commit as it was configured at the time and
so, as far as git is concerned, these are two different people. To tell it
that both identities are Jane's, we first record her in the identity
register. Let's call this file \c{identities} and place it next to the
repository:

\
$ cat ../identities
: 1
name: Jane Doe
email: jane@example.org
alias: J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
\

The register is a manifest list with one manifest per person. The
\c{name} and \c{email} values are Jane's preferred identity, the one we
want git to show, and the \c{alias} value is another identity of hers. We
will discuss the register format in more detail in \l{#register The
Identity Register}.

Now we can run \c{mailmap}, passing the path to the register:

\
$ mailmap ../identities

repository:         /src/hello
history identities: 2 (2 mapped)

+# Generated from the identity register by mailmap.
+Jane Doe <jane@example.org> <jane@example.org>
+Jane Doe <jane@example.org> J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>

update '/src/hello/.mailmap'? [y/n]:
\

Let's go through this output. The first two lines tell us which repository
is being updated and that both identities in its history belong to someone
in the register. Next comes the change to the \c{.mailmap} file in the
\c{diff(1)} notation, which in our case is the whole file since the
repository did not have one.

Each entry maps the identity on its right hand side to the preferred
identity on its left hand side. The second entry is straightforward: it maps
Jane's old identity, name and email, to the new one. Notice that the first
entry has only an email on its right hand side. Such an entry applies to
every name at this email and this is what takes care of the lowercase
\c{jane}.

Once we answer \c{y}, \c{mailmap} installs the new \c{.mailmap} and commits
it, printing each command as it executes it. The commit message lists the
entries that were added and removed:

\
$ git log -1 --format=%B
Update .mailmap

  add    Jane Doe <jane@example.org> <jane@example.org>
  add    Jane Doe <jane@example.org> J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
\

And if we now ask git to use the \c{.mailmap} file, it shows a single
person:

\
$ git log --use-mailmap --format='%aN <%aE>' | sort -u
Jane Doe <jane@example.org>
\

If we run \c{mailmap} again, it tells us that the \c{.mailmap} file is up
to date and exits without changing anything:

\
$ mailmap ../identities
'/src/hello/.mailmap' is up to date
\

\N|The commit made by \c{mailmap} is itself part of the history and its
author has to be in the register for the next update to succeed. In this
example we assume the repository is configured to commit as Jane Doe, who
is already there.|

If we also wanted to push the commit, then we would pass \c{--push}. And to
see what \c{mailmap} would do without changing anything, there is
\c{--print-only}, which prints the same plan to \c{stdout} and exits. See
\l{mailmap(1)} for the complete list of options.

\h1#register|The Identity Register|

The identity register is the only place where people and their identities
are recorded and everything \c{mailmap} writes to a \c{.mailmap} file comes
from it. It is normally kept under version control and changes to it are
reviewed the same way as changes to code.

\h#register-format|Format|

The register is a manifest list (see \l{bpkg#manifest-format Manifest
Format} for details on the format) with one manifest per person. For
example:

\
: 1
name: Jane Doe
email: jane@example.org
alias: J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
alias: <jdoe@example.com>
:
name: John Roe
email: john@example.org
\

The \c{name} and \c{email} values specify the person's preferred identity,
which is the identity the \c{.mailmap} entries map to. Both values are
required and each can only appear once.

The \c{alias} values specify the person's other identities. There can be
any number of them and each is either a name followed by an email in angle
brackets or an email in angle brackets alone. The first form is the
identity with this exact name and email. The second form is every identity
with this email whatever its name. In the above example, the second alias
covers any name Jane may have committed under with her
\c{jdoe@example.com} email.

The register also records how each person is listed in the \c{AUTHORS}
file of a repository, with the \c{authors} value that the \c{authors} tool
reads. \c{mailmap} only checks that this value is valid.

Any other manifest value is an error.

\h#register-matching|Matching|

To find the person an identity belongs to, \c{mailmap} follows the same
precedence git uses when applying a \c{.mailmap} file. First it looks for
an alias with this name and email. If there is none, then it looks for a
person with this email, either as the preferred email or as an email
alias. In both cases names and emails are compared case-insensitively. So,
for example, \c{jane <JANE@example.org>} belongs to Jane because of her
preferred email.

The name and email lookup taking precedence is what allows telling apart
two people who have committed with the same email. For example, if John
and Rick have both used a shared \c{build@example.org} email:

\
: 1
name: John Roe
email: john@example.org
alias: John <build@example.org>
:
name: Rick Poe
email: rick@example.org
alias: <build@example.org>
\

Here \c{John <build@example.org>} belongs to John while any other name at
\c{build@example.org} belongs to Rick.

\h#register-rules|Rules|

For the generated \c{.mailmap} to be unambiguous, each identity must belong
to at most one person. To guarantee this, the register must satisfy the
following rules and \c{mailmap} refuses to use a register that does not:

\ul|

\li|An email, preferred or alias, belongs to a single person.|

\li|An alias with a name and email belongs to a single person.|

\li|An alias with a name and email does not match another person's
preferred identity. Since such an alias is looked up first, it would take
that person's commits away from them.|

\li|An alias is not already covered by the person's own emails. For
example, the \c{Jane <jane@example.org>} alias adds nothing to the
\c{jane@example.org} preferred email.||

The diagnostics for each rule names the people involved. For example:

\
error: email 'j@example.io' belongs to multiple people in 'identities'
  info: 'Jane Doe' and 'John Roe'
\

Similarly, every identity in the history of a repository must belong to
someone. If some do not, then \c{mailmap} lists them and changes nothing:

\
error: history of '/src/hello' has identities not in 'identities'
  info: Stranger <stranger@example.net>
\

\h1#architecture|How mailmap Works|

In this chapter we look at how \c{mailmap} derives the \c{.mailmap} file
from the register and the history, how it makes sure the result is
correct, and in what order it changes things.

\h#architecture-identities|History Identities|

The identities of a repository are the commit authors along with the
\c{Co-authored-by} trailer values in the history of \c{HEAD}. They are read
as recorded, without applying the existing \c{.mailmap}, and so the
existing file has no effect on the one that replaces it. If a
\c{Co-authored-by} value is not a valid identity, then \c{mailmap} issues a
warning and skips it since git would not be able to map it either.

\h#architecture-entries|Entries|

For each history identity \c{mailmap} finds the person it belongs to and
then the entry that maps it. If the identity was matched with a name and
email alias, then the entry maps this exact name and email and git applies
it to nothing else:

\
Jane Doe <jane@example.org> J. Doe <jane@old.example.org>
\

If the identity was matched with an email, preferred or alias, then the
entry maps the email alone and git applies it to every name at this email:

\
Jane Doe <jane@example.org> <jdoe@example.com>
\

In this case the email is written as it appears in the register. The
preferred identity itself does not need an entry and none is written for
it.

The file starts with a comment that says where it comes from, followed by
the entries without duplicates and sorted bytewise. As a result, the same
history and register always produce the same file, byte for byte, and a
\c{.mailmap} that is up to date is left alone.

\h#architecture-proof|Proof|

The entries are derived by rules that are meant to match git's own, and to
make sure they do, \c{mailmap} asks git. Before changing anything, it
creates an empty repository that reads no global or system configuration,
places the generated file there, and passes every attributed history
identity to \c{git-check-mailmap(1)}. Each identity must map to the
preferred identity of the person the register attributes it to. If one
does not, then there is a bug in \c{mailmap} and it fails without changing
anything.

\h#architecture-phases|Phases|

An update proceeds in three phases. In the first phase \c{mailmap} prepares
every repository. This includes generating and proving the \c{.mailmap} file
as well as making sure the update can be installed, committed, and, if
requested, pushed. Specifically, the existing \c{.mailmap} must have no
uncommitted changes (since they would be lost), and the committer identity
must be configured (unless \c{--no-commit} is specified). For a push, as
well as with \c{--show-push}, the branch must also have an upstream and
must not be ahead of it (we will come back to this in a moment). If any
repository fails to prepare, then nothing is changed in any of them.

In the second phase \c{mailmap} prints the plan and asks for confirmation,
once for all the repositories. With \c{--print-only} the plan is printed to
\c{stdout} and \c{mailmap} stops at this point.

In the last phase \c{mailmap} installs each \c{.mailmap} by renaming a
complete temporary file over it, so the file is never observed partially
written. It then commits the file, and only this file, whatever else
happens to be staged. Finally, if requested, it pushes the commit to the
upstream of the current branch, explicitly and regardless of the
\c{push.default} setting. With \c{--show-push} the push command is printed
for us to examine the commit and run it ourselves.

The reason the branch must not be ahead of its upstream is that git pushes
every commit the upstream does not have. With the branch level with its
upstream, the push only carries the \c{.mailmap} commit. If the push fails,
for example, because the upstream has moved on in the meantime, then the
commit is kept and can be pushed once the branch is brought up to date.

\h1#all|Updating Many Repositories|

With \c{--all}, \c{mailmap} operates on every repository it finds under a
directory. For example, if we have cloned all the repositories of an
organization into \c{org/}:

\
$ mailmap --all --push identities org/
\

The search does not descend into a repository's working tree and so
repositories nested in it, such as submodules, are not visited. Subtrees
are part of the working tree of their repository and are not visited
either. Hidden directories are searched since a repository can itself be
hidden (\c{.github} is a common example). Linked worktrees are skipped with
a warning since their repository is visited through its main working tree.

The phases described in the previous chapter apply to all the repositories
together. In particular, every repository is prepared before any of them
is changed, which means that a single run reports the problems in all of
them and that a problem in one leaves all of them unchanged:

\
error: history of '/src/org/hello' has identities not in 'identities'
  info: Stranger <stranger@example.net>
error: 1 of 19 repositories failed, nothing changed
\

This also makes \c{--all --print-only} a convenient way to build up the
register for a new set of repositories: we run it, add the identities it
lists, and repeat until it prints the plan.

Once the update is confirmed, a failure to push one repository does not
prevent the remaining repositories from being updated and pushed. Such a
failure is diagnosed as it happens and \c{mailmap} exits with a non-zero
status once all the repositories are processed.
"
